|> Exercise
    handle=_x_square_x_cube_construction_

    Given a number line with $0$, $1$
    and a value $A > 0$ indicated,
    how can we construct the values $A^2$ and $A^3$
    as lengths, using only ruler and compass?

    |> Solution

        _Solution 1._ If $A > 1$,
        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_0.svg
        start by constructing a perpendicular to the $x$ axis
        (“the number line” = “the $x$ axis”) through $1$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_1.svg

        Take the intersection of this line with a
        circle of center $(0, 0)$ of radius $A$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_2_38deg.svg

        This gives us an angle $\theta$ such that $\sec\theta = A$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_3.svg

        Now it suffices to apply one of the “powers of
        secant” constructions to obtain powers of $A$,
        for example either of:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_4.svg

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_5.svg
            style=margin-bottom:0.5em;

        (Either of these constructiongs requires constructing
        perpendiculars, but we know how to do that.)

        If $A < 1$, on the other hand,
        do the same initial steps but with ‘$1$’ and ‘$A$’
        reversed, resulting in an angle $\theta$ for
        which $A = \cos\theta$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_7.svg

        We can then do a “powers of cosine” construction,
        that is symmetric to powers of secant, to
        find powers of $A$, for example:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_8.svg

        Though in this case, it bears discussing how we
        construct, say, this perpendicular:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_9.svg

        ...because this perpendicular must 

        __arrive__

        perpendicularly at a given line, not

        __depart__

        perpendicularly from a given line. 
        (In the order in which the construction unfolds,
        this perpendicular “originates” from its upper end,
        which is a situation that we have not encountered
        yet.)
        In this case, the procedure is to draw a circle,
        in orange below, whose center is the 
        starting point
        of the
        perpendicular, and that crosses the line-to-which-we-must-be-perpendicular
        in two places:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_10.svg

        Then use those crossings as the
        centers of two more circles of equal radius...

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_11.svg

        ...whose intersection gives us a second point
        (even two more points, if we use a radius
        different from the first orange circle), and the means to draw
        the perpendicular itself.

        |> Pause

        _Solution 2._ Regardless of whether $A < 1$ or $A > 1$,
        draw a perpendicular to the number line through $x = 1$
        and use a compass to report $A$ onto this line
        (here with $A < 1$):

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_13.svg

        This defines a an angle $\theta$ such that $A = \tan\theta$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_14.svg

        Then we can use any construction for powers of
        tan, for example this spiral construction:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_15.svg

        (Again, constructing the spiral means constructing
        a sequence of perpendiculars, but we can do that!)

        |> SolutionNote

            If $A < 1$ there is a clever construction,
            variant of solution 1, that one can use to
            construct powers of $A$ using only one compass
            stroke and one ruler stroke per power of $A$
            (after setting up the angle $\theta$):

            |> Image
                src=images/e5_x_square_x_cube_construction_16_v6.svg

            (No such thing exists for $A > 1$.)
            !! Curiously, there is no similar 
            !! construction for $A > 1$, all of
            !! which require three-compass-and-one-ruler-stroke constructions
            !! of perpendiculars to keep “reaching” the next
            !! power of $A$.

